  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe overall pattern remains mostly the same, with a ridge over \nthe central US and a trough over eastern Canada. This will allow\nfor quick hitting shortwaves and surface highs to push through \nthe area through the rest of the week. The first high will be \nover the area today, keeping us dry with temperatures near \nnormal for this time of year. The first shortwave will move in \nduring the late afternoon hours from the Great Lakes, bringing \nscattered rain showers into the overnight hours. With the \narrival of this shortwave occurring after peak heating, there \nisn't much surface instability available, but model soundings \nare showing some elevated instability so a few thunderstorms \ncannot be ruled out, especially in the evening hours. Behind \nthis shortwave, another surface high will build in from the \nGreat Lakes, keeping conditions dry and temps seasonable on \nThursday. \n\nModel guidance differs a bit for Friday with the timing and \nplacement of another stronger shortwave that is forecast to move \nthrough the NE US. Precip placement will depend on how far south the \nridge will allow the trough axis to dig. This shortwave should \ndevelop a surface low that will drag a cold front through the \nregion. Much cooler air is expected to filter in from Canada, with \nFriday night lows in the upper 40s to mid 50s.\n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nSaturday looks to be gorgeous as high pressure remains overhead. \nTemperatures are expected to be in the low to mid 70s with dewpoints \nin the low to mid 50s. The high will propagate eastward \nSaturday night into Sunday as a low pressures system barrels \nthrough the Great Lakes from the north central US. Scattered \nrain showers and storms are expected Sunday. If the track of the\nlow is to our north, we will be in the warm sector and could \nsee a ribbon of high instability build into the area from the SW\nbefore the low drags a cold front through the area. 0-6km bulk \nshear from the low and stronger mid to upper level winds from \nthe jet overhead could provide storm organization and, combined \nwith the instability, produce severe storms. Confidence in this \nis low at the moment given the track of the low is still \nuncertain and severe weather will be dependent on the low taking\na particular track. Either way, we will be monitoring how this \nsystem develops.\n\nBehind the low, a strong high pressure system will build in from the \nwest. This will bring NNW flow that will push Canadian air into the \nregion and keep temperatures slightly below normal and keep us dry \ninto mid-week.",
